Z||000006||pontiacs4me||08-31-2001||03:59 AM||||the hp/torque graph in the link I posted had some numbers for a 3.4 liter v6.  If you look where on the graph the torque numbers and the hp numbers cross each other its about 4500 rpm.  No matter what engine you have, whether its a briggs and stratton 5 horse, or a viper v10 or a 2.5 liter iron duke, or a 2.8, the horsepower and torque lines will always cross at 5252 rpm.  Thats because horsepower is a mathematical calculation from the torque that is measured from a dyno.  A dyno doesnt measure hp, it measures torque, then using the torque lb ft value at a given rpm, horsepower can be calculated.  <BR>I just thought that graph was funny, I looked at it the first time, and wondered why the hp numbers kept going up at the far right of the graph, when the torque was falling like the nasdaq, then I realized someone was drunk that day at work when they decided to put that graph on the website.  Im easily amused I guess.||||reg||1
